// form-find-ask.bml — ask the form-find service one question: one wire JSON on stdin, the wire's JSON answer on stdout.
//
// This is the cold-start client: `./fkwu observe/form-find-ask.bml` is a whole process (the runtime opened, the closure read), which is the
// cost the zero-process form avoids. The agents' normal lookup is the shell's own redirect into the spool, with no process begun:
//
//   SP=/tmp/form-find/$UID; printf '%s' '{"command":"defs fat-wire-call"}' > $SP/ask.$$.json
//   until [ -s $SP/ans.$$.json ]; do :; done; cat $SP/ans.$$.json; rm -f $SP/ans.$$.json     (docs/form-native-agent-tools.md)
//
// This door does the same in Form: it writes ask.<pid>-<ms>.json into the spool and rests on host_watch over the spool until
// ans.<pid>-<ms>.json arrives (the answer is renamed in, an event) or the deadline passes (FORM_FIND_DEADLINE_MS, default 3000), prints it
// and removes it. A service that is not alive (service.pid names no living process) is not waited for: the question is answered by the
// cold door, an index this process builds for itself (fpa-cold: form-find's index with the `path` verb taught; seconds, once), never by a
// host tool. A living service that does not answer in time is a named refusal.
// preflight-exec: forbidden
// preludes: form-stdlib/bml/form-path.bml form-stdlib/bml/stdin-source.bml
import form-stdlib/bml/form-path.bml;
import form-stdlib/bml/stdin-source.bml;
section [form.bml] {
 class FormFindAsk {
  def ffa-run() = print_str(fpa-one(fat-trim(stdin-text())));
 }
 ffa-run();
}
